      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "Let's start by visualizing the traveler's journey on a coordinate plane. We can place the starting point at the origin, (0, 0), and then move the traveler in the given directions.\n\n1. The traveler heads 18 miles north, so we move 18 units up along the y-axis. This puts us at (0, 18).\n\n2. Next, the traveler moves 11 miles west, so we move 11 units to the left along the x-axis. This puts us at (-11, 18).\n\n3. Then, the traveler moves 6 miles south, so we move 6 units down along the y-axis. This puts us at (-11, 12).\n\n4. Finally, the traveler moves 6 miles east, so we move 6 units to the right along the x-axis. This puts us at (-5, 12).\n\nNow, we can calculate the distance from the starting point to the final position using the distance formula:\n\nDistance = √[(x2 - x1)^2 + (y2 - y1)^2]\n\nIn this case, (x1, y1) is the starting point (0, 0) and (x2, y2) is the final position (-5, 12).\n\nDistance = √[(-5 - 0)^2 + (12 - 0)^2]\nDistance = √[(-5)^2 + (12)^2]\nDistance = √[25 + 144]\nDistance = √169\nDistance = 13 miles\n\nSo, the traveler was 13 miles from the starting point after these four legs of the journey.",
