    {
      "sample_index": 4,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to track the traveler's movements on a coordinate plane. We'll use the coordinates (0,0) to represent the starting point. Let's break down each leg of the journey:\n\n1. **First Leg:**\n   - The traveler heads 18 miles north.\n   - This moves the traveler from (0,0) to (0,18).\n\n2. **Second Leg:**\n   - The traveler then heads 11 miles west.\n   - This moves the traveler from (0,18) to (11,18).\n\n3. **Third Leg:**\n   - The traveler then heads 6 miles south.\n   - This moves the traveler from (11,18) to (11,12).\n\n4. **Fourth Leg:**\n   - The traveler finally heads 6 miles east.\n   - This moves the traveler from (11,12) to (17,12).\n\nNow, we need to determine the distance from the starting point (0,0) to the final position (17,12).\n\nTo find the distance between these two points, we use the distance formula:\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{(x_2 - x_1)^2 + (y_2 - y_1)^2}\n\\]\nwhere \\((x_1, y_1)\\) and \\((x_2, y_2)\\) are the coordinates of the two points.\n\nSubstituting the coordinates (0,0) and (17,12) into the formula, we get:\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{(17 - 0)^2 + (12 - 0)^2} = \\sqrt{17^2 + 12^2}\n\\]\n\nCalculating the squares:\n\\[\n17^2 = 289\n\\]\n\\[\n12^2 = 144\n\\]\n\nAdding these values together:\n\\[\n289 + 144 = 433\n\\]\n\nTaking the square root of the sum:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{433}\n\\]\n\nSo, the distance from the starting point to the final position is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\sqrt{433}}\n\\] miles.",
