    "text": "To determine how many miles the traveler is from the starting point after completing the four legs of the journey, we need to calculate the net displacement in both the north-south and east-west directions.\n\n1. **North-South Displacement:**\n   - The traveler first heads 18 miles north.\n   - Then, the traveler heads 6 miles south.\n   - The net north-south displacement is:\n     \\[\n     18 \\text{ miles north} - 6 \\text{ miles south} = 12 \\text{ miles north}\n     \\]\n\n2. **East-West Displacement:**\n   - The traveler first heads 11 miles west.\n   - Then, the traveler heads 6 miles east.\n   - The net east-west displacement is:\n     \\[\n     11 \\text{ miles west} - 6 \\text{ miles east} = 5 \\text{ miles west}\n     \\]\n\n3. **Distance from the Starting Point:**\n   - The net displacement in the north-south direction is 12 miles north.\n   - The net displacement in the east-west direction is 5 miles west.\n   - The distance from the starting point is the hypotenuse of a right triangle with legs of 12 miles and 5 miles.\n   - Using the Pythagorean theorem:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Distance} = \\sqrt{(12 \\text{ miles})^2 + (5 \\text{ miles})^2} = \\sqrt{144 + 25} = \\sqrt{169} = 13 \\text{ miles}\n     \\]\n\nThus, the traveler is \\(\\boxed{13}\\) miles from the starting point after completing the four legs of the journey.",
